	spec: './install (bash, repo root) sets up a checkout, doing only what is missing, safe to run again. It shows a checklist ([x] done, [ ] missing): bun (on PATH or ~/.bun/bin), bun install (node_modules), ~/.local/bin/hal as a symlink to this checkout\'s run, ~/.local/bin in PATH. Everything done: it says "Everything in order. Start with: hal" and exits. Otherwise it asks once "Set up Hal? [Y/n]" (-y answers yes; any other option is refused with exit 1 before anything changes), then: Bun through the official curl https://bun.sh/install | bash; bun install --cwd <checkout>; the symlink (ln -sfn, replacing a link to another checkout); the PATH line appended with a "# Added by hal installer" comment to the login shell\'s config (zsh ~/.zshenv, bash ~/.bash_profile on macOS or ~/.bashrc, fish ~/.config/fish/config.fish with fish_add_path; other shells get the line printed), skipped when the config already mentions .local/bin outside comments. It ends with how to start (hal, after a new shell when PATH changed). Deliberately minimal, unlike the old Hal installer (artifact old-install): no git, ripgrep, Homebrew, config file or local-only mode. Test (src/install.test.ts) in a temp HOME: link and one PATH line, a second run adds nothing, a PATH with the bin dir is in order, an unknown option changes nothing. ./run runs ./install first when node_modules is missing, saying "Dependencies are missing (<path>/node_modules): running ./install first."; without dependencies Hal would start but its web client could not build.',
